{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"a516661a05e31734","name":"card_event_refit_bia","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/BrothersInArms/events/card_event_refit_bia","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"REFIT","text":"Choose one - draw a Spitfire from your deck OR give a Spitfire Blitz.","flavorText":"During the Battle of Britain, a key difference in engineering between the German Messerschmitt 109 and the British Supermarine Spitfire and Hawker Hurricane was their fuel delivery systems. The German fighters had a direct fuel injection, allowing them to perform extreme negative G maneuvers without starving the engine of fuel. This gave the ME-109 an advantage in quickly diving away from combat. On the other hand, the British fighters used direct carburetors, which were sensitive to engine cut-outs due to fuel flooding during negative G maneuvers. To work around this issue, British pilots developed the tactic of flipping their aircraft in a half-roll before diving, converting the maneuver into a positive G one and avoiding engine cut-outs. This problem persisted in British planes until 1941 when Miss Tilly Shilling introduced a simple but effective solution using a washer-type device to help alleviate the fuel and float level fluctuations that caused the engine to cut out. While it didn't completely solve the problem, it considerably improved the situation.","faction":"Britain","type":"order","rarity":"Rare","cost":1,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_refit_bia","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_refit_bia_title","SourceString":"REFIT","LocalizedString":"REFIT"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_refit_bia_text","SourceString":"Choose one - draw a Spitfire from your deck OR give a Spitfire Blitz.","LocalizedString":"Choose one - draw a Spitfire from your deck OR give a Spitfire Blitz."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_refit_bia_history","SourceString":"During the Battle of Britain, a key difference in engineering between the German Messerschmitt 109 and the British Supermarine Spitfire and Hawker Hurricane was their fuel delivery systems. The German fighters had a direct fuel injection, allowing them to perform extreme negative G maneuvers without starving the engine of fuel. This gave the ME-109 an advantage in quickly diving away from combat. On the other hand, the British fighters used direct carburetors, which were sensitive to engine cut-outs due to fuel flooding during negative G maneuvers. To work around this issue, British pilots developed the tactic of flipping their aircraft in a half-roll before diving, converting the maneuver into a positive G one and avoiding engine cut-outs. This problem persisted in British planes until 1941 when Miss Tilly Shilling introduced a simple but effective solution using a washer-type device to help alleviate the fuel and float level fluctuations that caused the engine to cut out. While it didn't completely solve the problem, it considerably improved the situation.","LocalizedString":"During the Battle of Britain, a key difference in engineering between the German Messerschmitt 109 and the British Supermarine Spitfire and Hawker Hurricane was their fuel delivery systems. The German fighters had a direct fuel injection, allowing them to perform extreme negative G maneuvers without starving the engine of fuel. This gave the ME-109 an advantage in quickly diving away from combat. On the other hand, the British fighters used direct carburetors, which were sensitive to engine cut-outs due to fuel flooding during negative G maneuvers. To work around this issue, British pilots developed the tactic of flipping their aircraft in a half-roll before diving, converting the maneuver into a positive G one and avoiding engine cut-outs. This problem persisted in British planes until 